  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 935 AM MST Sun Nov 1 2026\n\nUnsettled weather is forecast for the short-term. Plentiful\nmoisture has moved over the region, exhibited by our 12z (!) RAOB,\nwhich collected 1.21\" of PWs. Normal for early Nov is about 0.4\".\nThis is courtesy of the subtropical jet being pulled northward\nahead of a deep longwave trough moving into the Great Basin today.\nWe'll be in the right entrance region of the jet with good speed\nshear and modest instability. Only about 500 J/kg of SBCAPE are\nmodeled for this afternoon with higher values to the east into SE\nNM. Excellent 0-6km shear of 50kts is modeled by the REFS later \ntoday as the jet streak moves overhead. All the cloud cover \nstreaming overhead may inhibit how unstable we get later today, \nbut the favorable jet/frontal dynamics should help overcome that \nin terms of convective initiation. \n\nThe Pacific front will push through S NM this evening with a wave\nof showers and storms ahead of it, forming what appears to be a \nsquall line. Strong-severe wind gusts are possible from this \nconvective line as it progresses W to E into the overnight. SPC\nhas increased our severe risk for today/tonight with a portion of\nOtero and Sierra counties now in a SLGT for damaging winds and an\nisolated tornado. 0-1km SRH should exceed 100 m2/s2 in some areas\nthrough the evening, favoring northern areas for the marginal\ntornado threat. Eastern NM is most likely to see tornadoes through\ntonight. The rain/storm threat will diminish for far west TX as\nthe best dynamics track to the northeast. \n\nFlood Watch remains in effect for the Gila through the evening\nwhere moisture and jet dynamics are most favorable. Repeated\nrounds of rain are forecast with isolated amounts of 1-2\" and a\nfew instances of flash flooding, especially in areas that have\nreceived heavy rainfall recently. The flood threat lowers as you\nhead south and east. Rain chances largely end by the early AM Mon.\n\nBreezy to strong synoptic winds are forecast as well with this\nsystem, increasing to near advisory level in the Gila this\nafternoon, shifting E overnight to the San Andres/Organs and then\nthe Sacs. The strongest winds are expected in the Sacs with gusts\nto 60-70 mph possible along ridges and east slopes into the day on\nMon. Wind headlines have been posted to reflect these hazards.\nElsewhere, breezy conditions are expected with gusts to around 40\nmph, continuing into Mon mainly E of the RGV. \n\nDry and calmer winds after Mon with seasonal temps under quasi-\nzonal flow.",
